QA3 = [
# ---------- Section 7: Payments (second part) ----------
(7, "How does the POS learn that an M-Pesa payment succeeded?",
 "After a successful STK push the checkout view model polls GET /payments/{payment_transaction_id}, honouring the server's poll_after_ms (2 s by default) and stopping after a two-minute ceiling. A SUCCESS response carries the receipt and the sale's new payment_status, so the till can print. If the window closes with the sale still PENDING, the cashier is told it will update rather than being shown a failure.",
 "orangemain/shared/src/commonMain/kotlin/org/whoismacy/mainconsumapp/ui/screens/checkout/CheckoutViewModel.kt (DEFAULT_POLL_MS, POLL_TIMEOUT_MS); backend/src/routes/payments.ts"),
(7, "Why polling instead of WebSockets for v1?",
 "The scale is a small shop fleet, and the POS is on flaky mobile data where a long-lived socket is one more thing to reconnect. Polling for two minutes covers the interactive case, and terminal status changes ride the sales slice of GET /sync/pull, which is the guaranteed eventual-delivery path even if the app was killed or the device went offline mid-payment. WebSocket/SSE push is explicitly deferred to v1.5.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §4.3; orangemain/.../data/sync/SyncService.kt (pull applies sale status changes)"),
(7, "What exactly does the payment status poll return?",
 "{status, result_code, result_desc, mpesa_receipt_number, sale: {id, payment_status, mpesa_receipt}}. The sale block is what the till applies locally; the transaction block is for diagnostics. The server is the only writer of payment_status, so the client never guesses a payment outcome.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §4.3; backend/src/routes/payments.ts (status route)"),
(7, "What if the cashier leaves the checkout screen before the callback lands?",
 "Nothing is lost: the sale stays PENDING locally and the server settles it when Daraja calls back. The next sync pull re-sends the changed sale with payment_status=PAID and the receipt, and the POS updates its local row. Payment state is server-owned; the polling loop is just a convenience for the cashier standing at the counter.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §3.2, §4.3; backend/src/routes/sync.ts (sales status slice)"),
(7, "Why does an STK push require the sale to be synced first?",
 "The server has to own the payment_transaction: it validates that the sale exists in the token's branch, recomputes the total, and enforces the in-flight check against rows a device cannot spoof. STK needs connectivity anyway, so requiring the sale upstream adds no practical constraint while removing an entire class of trust problems.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §4.1, §6 decision 2; backend/src/routes/payments.ts (preconditions)"),
(7, "Why are payment transactions server-created with server ids?",
 "A device-generated id would let a till replay or collide transactions and would make rate limiting and in-flight detection unreliable. The server mints UUIDv7 payment_transaction rows on Daraja accept and returns the id + checkout_request_id; the device's only identity is its token, which the server resolves to device_id and branch_id.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §4.1, §6 decision 3; backend/src/routes/payments.ts"),
(7, "What is the late-success problem and how is it handled?",
 "The timeout sweeper marks a PENDING attempt FAILED after five minutes so a lost callback cannot hang the till forever. If a SUCCESS callback then arrives for that FAILED attempt, the server never auto-flips the sale: it queues a reconciliation row with the receipt and payload reference. Money moved, but only a human can decide whether to apply it, refund it, or discard it.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts (sweeper, late success branch); backend/src/routes/reconciliation.ts; API_CONTRACT.md §4.4, §4.5"),
(7, "What can an OWNER do with a reconciliation row?",
 "GET /reconciliation/payments lists open flags; POST /reconciliation/payments/{id}/resolve takes APPLY_TO_SALE, MARK_REFUND, or DISCARD. APPLY_TO_SALE is the single exceptional path that can force a sale to PAID outside the callback flow, and it writes an audit row naming the actor. That keeps the ledger provable: every state change has a named cause.",
 "backend/src/routes/reconciliation.ts; API_CONTRACT.md §4.5"),
(7, "What rate limits protect the STK endpoint?",
 "Per contract: one request per 3 s per sale and ten per minute per device, both returning 429. Together with the PENDING check (409 STK_IN_PROGRESS) these stop a double-tap from double-charging a customer and stop a buggy client from hammering Daraja. A legitimate retry is allowed only after the previous attempt is terminal or older than five minutes.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §4.1; backend/src/lib/rate-limit.ts; backend/src/routes/payments.ts"),
(7, "Which payment errors reach the cashier, and are they retryable?",
 "INVALID_MSISDN (422) and AMOUNT_MISMATCH (422) are fixed by editing the input; STK_IN_PROGRESS (409) means wait for the current prompt; DARAJA_UNAVAILABLE (502) is retryable. The POS surfaces each as a distinct message instead of a generic failure, because 'wrong number' and 'Safaricom is down' call for different cashier behaviour.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §4.1; orangemain/.../ui/screens/checkout/CheckoutViewModel.kt (STK error handling)"),
(7, "How is the payment flow idempotent end to end?",
 "Three layers: mutation endpoints accept Idempotency-Key and replay the stored response; payment_transaction state transitions are write-once (PENDING → terminal, receipt written once); and duplicate Daraja callbacks are no-ops keyed on the transaction's state, with a unique reconciliation index preventing duplicate queue rows. Daraja retries deliveries, so every layer has to be safe to repeat.",
 "backend/src/lib/idempotency.ts; backend/migrations/002_reconciliation_and_heartbeat.sql; backend/test/payments.test.ts"),
(7, "Why convert cents to whole shillings only at the Daraja boundary?",
 "The database and API speak integer cents everywhere, so totals never lose precision. Daraja's STK API takes integer shillings, so the conversion happens in one place server-side after amount validation. Keeping the conversion at the edge means no client can present a different number to Daraja than the sale the server validated.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §4.1; backend/src/routes/payments.ts; backend/src/payments/daraja.ts"),

# ---------- Section 8: Reports & BI ----------
(8, "What report endpoints were added this round?",
 "Two read-only aggregates: GET /branches/{id}/reports/sales-trend?days=N returns one point per UTC day (revenue + count) for a single branch, and GET /reports/branch-comparison?days=N returns revenue, sale count, low-stock count, and active-device count per branch for the caller's scope. Both are additive v1 endpoints; neither touches entity rows.",
 "backend/src/routes/stats.ts; API_CONTRACT.md §2 (BI reports)"),
(8, "Why put reports in stats instead of adding fields to Branch?",
 "The Branch entity has to stay lean and syncable; aggregate fields would be denormalized caches that drift the moment a sale lands. Computing on demand from the ledger means the numbers are always correct by construction, and the POS never has to carry financial aggregates it doesn't need.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §2 ('the Branch entity itself stays lean'); backend/src/routes/stats.ts"),
(8, "How exactly is the report window defined?",
 "Whole UTC days ending today: from = today 00:00 UTC minus (days − 1) days, to = tomorrow 00:00 UTC, with from inclusive and to exclusive. Using UTC everywhere keeps the window identical for the server, the stored timestamps, and tests; days is validated 1–90 with a default of 7 via a zod schema, so a nonsense value is a 400 before any SQL runs.",
 "backend/src/routes/stats.ts (windowBounds, REPORT_WINDOW); API_CONTRACT.md §2"),
(8, "Why are only PAID sales counted?",
 "Revenue has to be provable. PENDING means the M-Pesa outcome is unknown and FAILED means no money arrived, so counting either would inflate the dashboard. When reconciliation later applies a late success the sale becomes PAID and the day's total corrects itself automatically on the next read.",
 "backend/src/routes/stats.ts (FILTER (WHERE payment_status = 'PAID')); API_CONTRACT.md §2"),
(8, "Why zero-fill days with no sales?",
 "Charts are drawn per day position, so a missing day would compress the time axis and make a quiet Tuesday look like a trend. The server returns every day in the window with zeros, which also makes the client's job trivial and the response shape stable for tests.",
 "backend/src/routes/stats.ts (zero-filled points); backend/test/stats.test.ts"),
(8, "How is branch scoping enforced on reports?",
 "sales-trend resolves the caller's claims and asserts the path branch is in token.branch_ids (OWNER implicitly has all) before running the query, failing with 403 BRANCH_FORBIDDEN. branch-comparison filters the SQL to the caller's branch list, with OWNER passing null to mean 'all'. Scope comes from the token only; a query parameter cannot widen it.",
 "backend/src/routes/stats.ts (assertBranchScope, branchIds); API_CONTRACT.md §1.3"),
(8, "What does branch-comparison return and how is it ordered?",
 "Per branch: name, revenue for the window, paid-sale count, low-stock item count, and non-revoked device count, sorted by revenue descending so the strongest branch leads the list. The admin renders it as proportional bars, which makes relative performance readable without any chart library.",
 "backend/src/routes/stats.ts; API_CONTRACT.md §2; orangeadmin/.../screens/main/ReportsScreen.kt"),
(8, "Why is low stock defined as <= 5 units?",
 "Five units is the threshold where a shelf is close enough to empty that a restock cannot wait for the next delivery cycle at the volumes these shops run. The constant is shared by the dashboard and the comparison endpoint so 'low stock' means one thing everywhere rather than drifting between screens.",
 "backend/src/routes/stats.ts (LOW_STOCK_THRESHOLD); API_CONTRACT.md §2"),
(8, "Why exclude revoked devices from devices_count?",
 "The count is a fleet-health signal: how many tills are expected to be transacting. Revoked rows stay in the table for audit and receipt history but are not part of the fleet, so counting them would overstate coverage.",
 "backend/src/routes/stats.ts; API_CONTRACT.md §2"),
(8, "How are the reports rendered in the admin app?",
 "ReportsScreen draws the sales trend as a bar chart on a Compose Canvas, one bar per UTC day, with the day's total above and the date label below; the branch comparison renders proportional bars inside cards. Range chips re-trigger the fetch through MainViewModel.loadSalesTrend(branchId, days) and loadBranchComparison(days).",
 "orangeadmin/.../screens/main/ReportsScreen.kt; orangeadmin/.../viewmodels/MainViewModel.kt"),
(8, "Why offer 7/14/30-day chips when the API allows 1–90?",
 "Those three cover the review rhythms actually used: week, fortnight, month. The API window stayed general so a future screen or export can ask for anything up to 90 days without a server change, and the UI stays clean.",
 "orangeadmin/.../screens/main/ReportsScreen.kt (window chips); backend/src/routes/stats.ts (REPORT_WINDOW)"),
(8, "What did the report tests cover?",
 "test/stats.test.ts exercises the trend endpoint (points, zero-fill, PAID-only filtering), branch scoping for a scoped manager, and the comparison aggregation including low-stock and device counts, as part of the 35/35 suite. Writing the tests first is what caught the from-inclusive/to-exclusive boundary.",
 "backend/test/stats.test.ts; CHANGELOG.md §3.6, §7"),
(8, "Is there a performance concern with the aggregates?",
 "The comparison query aggregates in SQL in one pass per request rather than one query per branch, and the trend query groups by day in SQL. At shop scale that is fine; the known backlog item is the N+1 on branch and product listing endpoints, which is a separate path from reports.",
 "backend/src/routes/stats.ts; CHANGELOG.md §8 item 4"),

# ---------- Section 9: Admin app UI & settings ----------
(9, "What is Settings -> Payment for?",
 "It is a local configuration screen for the Daraja values an operator needs at hand: paybill number, buy-goods till, consumer key, consumer secret, passkey, and shortcode. It shows a draft form, validates nothing beyond presence, and saves through SettingsPreferencesRepository to a Dialog+ preferences DataStore. The screen does not call the backend at all.",
 "orangeadmin/.../screens/main/SettingsPaymentScreen.kt; orangeadmin/.../domain/AdminData.kt (PaymentSettings)"),
(9, "How are those Daraja fields stored, and are they synced?",
 "In a preferencesDataStore named settings_preferences under keys like mpesa_paybill_number and mpesa_passkey; saved values are trimmed and isConfigured is true when any field is non-blank. They are deliberately never sent to the server or written into the API payloads: production credentials belong on the server, and the device copy is a local operational aid.",
 "orangeadmin/.../repository/SettingsPreferencesRepository.kt; orangeadmin/.../ntwk/NetworkService.kt (no payment-settings route); API_CONTRACT.md §5"),
(9, "Why DataStore instead of SharedPreferences?",
 "DataStore gives asynchronous, transactional reads and writes exposed as a Flow, so the form can bind to state without blocking the main thread and a half-written save cannot corrupt the file. SharedPreferences' synchronous apply/commit model is exactly the kind of main-thread I/O Compose screens are trying to avoid.",
 "orangeadmin/.../repository/SettingsPreferencesRepository.kt (preferencesDataStore)"),
(9, "How did the admin app's navigation change this round?",
 "A Reports destination was added to MainScreenNavigationItems with MainRoutes plus a new outline_bar_chart drawable, so Reports sits alongside branches and settings in the bottom navigation. BranchDetail continues to hold per-branch drill-down, including the device share sheet.",
 "orangeadmin/.../domain/MainScreenNavigationItems.kt; orangeadmin/.../nav/MainRoutes.kt; orangeadmin/.../res/drawable/outline_bar_chart_24.xml"),
(9, "How does MainViewModel keep screen state coherent after a write?",
 "It exposes refresh entry points - refreshAll, refreshDashboard(showLoading), refreshBranches, refreshInventory - and the report loaders loadSalesTrend(branchId, days) / loadBranchComparison(days). Screens call the matching refresh after a mutation, so lists and stats reload from the server instead of showing a stale pre-write snapshot; this is the behaviour fixed in 673cda9.",
 "orangeadmin/.../viewmodels/MainViewModel.kt; CHANGELOG.md §4, §2"),
(9, "Why is the admin app online-only while the POS is offline-first?",
 "The offline queue is the POS's job: a till must keep selling when the network drops. Admin actions are privileged, audited, and changing shared state, so a stale offline write is a correctness risk for everyone; the admin tolerates showing the last loaded data but mutations need the server. Keeping the split explicit also halves the sync surface.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §6 decision 7; CHANGELOG.md §8 item 6"),
(9, "How does the device register expose liveness?",
 "Each device row shows last_seen_at from heartbeats: 'never' when there is none, a relative age otherwise, and a stale highlight once it exceeds 24 hours. The POS heartbeat also carries outbox_pending, so a device that is connected but not draining its queue is visible as a diagnostic rather than a mystery.",
 "orangeadmin/.../screens/main/BranchDetailScreen.kt; backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (heartbeat); CHANGELOG.md §3.3"),
(9, "How does credential transfer from admin to POS work?",
 "The admin's device sheet renders the provisioned device's credentials as a QR code plus copyable human-readable text; the POS setup screen accepts either a paste or a QR scan through a shared parsing utility. The payload is parsed by the same function on both sides and covered by unit tests, so a scanning mismatch fails in tests rather than at a shop counter.",
 "orangeadmin/.../screens/main/BranchDetailScreen.kt (share sheet); orangemain/.../data/remote (shared parser); CHANGELOG.md §4, §5"),
(9, "Why is the credential share shown as QR plus text?",
 "The QR is fast when both devices are present, and the text is the fallback when a camera is unavailable, the screen is cracked, or the admin is provisioning remotely over the phone. Making the text the canonical payload and the QR a rendering of it means one format, one parser, one test.",
 "orangemain/... (QrPayloadTest-style unit tests); CHANGELOG.md §4"),
(9, "What invariants does the repository layer keep?",
 "AdminRepository wraps every network call in an apiCall helper returning Result<T>, maps DTOs to domain types (toSalesTrend, toBranchComparison), and centralises auth/refresh. Screens therefore never see raw HTTP, and a token refresh or an error shape changes in one place. The report DTO mapping also tolerates a missing days field by falling back to the number of points.",
 "orangeadmin/.../repository/AdminRepository.kt (apiCall, toSalesTrend, toBranchComparison)"),
(9, "How are report queries typed in the network layer?",
 "Retrofit-style declarations with @Query(\"days\") on both endpoints and @GET paths matching the contract exactly (/branches/{id}/reports/sales-trend and /reports/branch-comparison). Keeping the paths in one interface makes contract drift visible in a single diff.",
 "orangeadmin/.../ntwk/NetworkService.kt"),
(9, "What would you improve in the reports UI next?",
 "A share/export of the comparison (CSV or image) and a per-branch drill-down from a bar tap are the obvious next steps; both need only UI work on top of existing endpoints. The larger unfinished piece is the reconciliation screen, which is already the top admin backlog item because its API exists.",
 "CHANGELOG.md §8 items 1-2"),

# ---------- Section 10: Process, docs, verification ----------
(10, "What changed in API_CONTRACT this round?",
 "Additive v1 updates: the POST /devices/heartbeat payload and last_seen semantics, credential sharing for devices (share text + QR), the two BI report endpoints with their window rules, and the reconciliation endpoints. The document remains a draft until sign-off per the contract-first workflow, but every shipped route now has a section.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md (draft header, §1.2 heartbeat, §2 BI reports, §4.5 reconciliation)"),
(10, "Why is the contract locked before endpoints and UI?",
 "Every artifact - migrations, routes, repositories, Compose screens, tests - derives from the same shapes. Locking shapes and rules first means an endpoint or screen cannot quietly invent a field, and sign-off happens once instead of per layer. It is the step-gated workflow this project has followed since Step 2.",
 "DATA_CONTRACT.md v1 (locked); API_CONTRACT.md; CHANGELOG.md §8 item 7"),
(10, "What was done to the proposal document this round?",
 "project-description-revised.md was rewritten to match the implementation, covering the 20 approved revision items: sync hardening, reports, heartbeat, credential transfer, the settings screen, and the stated gaps. The PDF is still the old export and needs a manual Google Docs re-export, which is recorded as an open item rather than silently ignored.",
 "project-description-revised.md; CHANGELOG.md §6, §8"),
(10, "What does the verification matrix look like?",
 "Backend typecheck passes; the backend suite runs 35/35 green against local PostgreSQL 16; orangeadmin assembles debug and passes its unit tests; orangemain's androidApp assembles debug. The record lives in CHANGELOG.md so the claim is checkable rather than remembered.",
 "CHANGELOG.md §7"),
(10, "How is the test database handled?",
 "A private PG16 cluster at 127.0.0.1:55432 (database orange_test) stood up with initdb + pg_ctl because neither Docker nor a system Postgres service is available. test/helpers.ts assumes the schema exists and only truncates, migrations are applied beforehand, and the suite runs serialized because files share the database. It truncates app_user, so the owner login is reseeded after manual test runs.",
 "backend/test/helpers.ts; CHANGELOG.md §7"),
(10, "What is the single biggest risk before a live demo?",
 "Daraja. The sandbox app does not have the STK product enabled (404.001.03), and the callback URL must carry the secret query parameter. Both are configuration, not code, which is why they are tracked as an explicit P0 in the backlog instead of being buried in a bug list.",
 "CHANGELOG.md §8 item 1; API_CONTRACT.md §4.1, §4.2"),
(10, "What is deliberately still open?",
 "The reconciliation admin UI (API done, screen deferred), profile editing (endpoint semantics defined, UI pending), the N+1 on branch/product listings, the catalog category-deletion race, strict cashier branch membership, and the proposal PDF re-export. Each is listed in the changelog with its status, so 'done' and 'deferred' are distinguishable in a review.",
 "CHANGELOG.md §8"),
(10, "Why is the working tree still uncommitted?",
 "Because the project runs on contract-first, step-gated delivery: changes are committed only when asked, after the user has seen the verification verdict. That keeps each commit a reviewable unit rather than a snapshot of whatever happened to be on disk.",
 "CHANGELOG.md §8 item 7"),
(10, "If asked 'what makes this system distributed and real-time?', what is the answer?",
 "Distributed: many offline tills with their own local databases converge through an append-only, last-write-wins sync with clock-skew guards and audited dropped writes. Real-time: M-Pesa STK settlements flow back via callbacks, polling, and the sync pull watermark, and the admin sees fleet liveness through heartbeats and daily aggregates - all without a single point of sale blocking on the network.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts; orangemain/.../data/sync/SyncService.kt; backend/src/routes/payments.ts; backend/src/routes/stats.ts"),
]
